# =====================================================================
# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere in the repository.
#
# Why this file exists — CRLF has caused real breakage here, not
# hypothetical breakage:
#
#   1. Deployment targets are Linux. A .sh file with CRLF fails at the
#      shebang line, because the kernel reads the interpreter path as
#      "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and cannot find it.
#   2. A CRLF in an env value leaks the \r into container environment
#      variables. PG_USER=adm\r makes pg_isready -U $'adm\r' hang until
#      it times out; a POSTGRES_PASSWORD with \r is written into initdb
#      and every service that authenticates against it fails, with no
#      visible cause.
#   3. The evidence is in the tree: roughly 120 defensive `sed 's/\r//g'`
#      / `tr -d '\r'` lines across the deploy scripts, plus three
#      dedicated fix-sh-format.sh helpers. Those were added after the
#      fact, not as a precaution.
#   4. Mixed endings destroy cross-stack diffs. Comparing the same file
#      between the amd / nvidia / arm64 stacks reports *every* line as
#      changed, burying the real platform differences.
#
# NOTE: this policy covers version-controlled files only. The runtime
# env files (<stack>/.env, <module>/.env, tiger-tuning.env) are
# gitignored — a user may still paste CRLF into them from Windows or
# SMB, so the defensive \r stripping in the deploy scripts must stay.
# Do not treat it as dead code after normalization.
# =====================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must be excluded from EOL conversion. The repository
# currently tracks none, but `text=auto` corrupts any binary it
# misdetects as text, so the guard is kept ahead of need.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
